n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\rho (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\rho (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}[r]_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\, \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the object is $Q=\\pi .$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 false] [17592186360454 1074 "First, notice that the region $D$ represents a half-annulus enclosed between the circles of the radiuses $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}