 use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/4f/4f7b1647d893512605ee1a2e9cee3e5511a00dc7ad9b1765fff1d0ba0710895f.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{3}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(81-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =162(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the quarter-disk is $M=81\\pi .$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 false] [17592186360486 1074 "The mass of a quarter-disk $D$ of radius $3$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/4f/4f7b1647d893512605ee1a2e9cee3e5511a00dc7ad9b1765fff1d0ba0710895f.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{3}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(81-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =162(0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the quarter-disk is $M=81\\pi .$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 true] [17592186360489 1074 "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ not both zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B=O.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A$ and $B$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & -3{x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1} & {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} & {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & -2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} & -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 