umber shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =3+2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the first quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.588.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{13}$ and $\\theta =0.588$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{13}\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.588\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 false] [17592186372377 1074 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =3+2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the first quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ≈0.588.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{13}$ and $\\theta =0.588$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{13}\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.588\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 true] [17592186372384 1074 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =3+3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}