& =9\\,500 \\\\ 325+{t}_{o} & =475 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =150\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of sugar in the vat at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(150) & =300{\\left(\\frac{325}{325+150}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =300{\\left(\\frac{13}{19}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈65.75\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139538228 17592186576411 true] [17592186374875 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=-3C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(-3C) \\\\ 2x+2y{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =-2x \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{x}{y})}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." 13194139538228 17592186576411 false] [17592186374875 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=-3C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\f