izontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\frac{2}{x}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139538228 17592186576411 false] [17592186378422 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x}\\right)}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\frac{2}{x}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139538228 17592186576411 true] [17592186378705 1074 "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the