egin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)(\\frac{2}{3})+0(-36) \\\\ 0\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})+2(-36)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -72\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 false] [17592186390985 1074 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+9\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+0(-36) \\\\ 0\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+2(-36)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -72\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 true] [17592186390992 1074 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2(\\frac{1}{3})+0(6) \\\\ 0(\\frac{1}{3})+1(6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 false] [17592186390992 1074 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+0(6) \\\\ 0\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+1(6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 true] [17592186391624 1074 "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable 