se derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 false] [17592186401866 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 true] [17592186401915 1071 "![](images/c0/c091fc5d742ddca3090725974f00ff7da77b1530d601bb82e288518b27970b0a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." 13194139538228 17592186576411 false] [17592186401915 1071 "![](images/c0/c091fc5d742ddca3090725974f00ff7da77b1530d601bb82e288518b27970b0a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." 13194139538228 17592186576411 true] [17592186401915 1074 "The horizontal asympt