n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,2y,\\,2z-x-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,2y,\\,(2x-y+6)-x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,2y,\\,x-2y+6⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x-2y-2(x-2y+6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{3}}(2y-12)\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-6y]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538228 17592186576411 false] [17592186421241 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1⟩}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=3,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\underbrace{⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing norma